name conceptnet-assertions
# One edge on every line, five fields parted by tabs, no header row. ConceptNet's documentation (Downloads):
#   "The five fields of each line are: The URI of the whole edge; The relation expressed by the edge; The node at the
#    start of the edge; The node at the end of the edge; A JSON structure of additional information about the edge"
# The edge is its start, its relation and its end.
#
# (URI hierarchy) "Every object in ConceptNet has a URI that is structured like a path"; "Concept URIs contain the
# text of the concept, with spaces replaced by underscores"; a concept has "the initial /c", "a part that indicates
# its language", "a part with the concept text", and "an optional fourth component gives the part of speech". A URI
# is ConceptNet's address for a term, a key: the term is the text itself, the word any other witness speaks of, and
# what the address says of it (its language, its part of speech) is said of the term. /c/en/ice_cream/n is the
# term "ice cream", of which ConceptNet says en and n. A relation's URI, /r/IsA, is the relation IsA.
# The edge's own URI, and the dataset, licence and process the JSON names, are ConceptNet's bookkeeping.
#
# (Edges) "sources: the sources that, when combined, say that this assertion should be true": each "contributor"
# among them is a witness of its own and attests the edge. An edge that names no contributor is attested by ConceptNet.
match assertions.csv
grammar table
columns uri rel start end json
paths / _
address start 3 says 2 4
address end 3 says 2 4
address rel 2
omit dataset license process
specifics

claims
subject in start
predicate in rel
object in end
json json
witnesses sources contributor
